30078125, 0.0194854736328125, 0.01568603515625, 0.0286407470703125, -0.0017995834350585938, -0.01898193359375, -0.017974853515625, -0.00048613548278808594, -0.00182342529296875, -0.0079803466796875, 0.0250396728515625, 0.007686614990234375, 0.0135650634765625, -0.0197296142578125, 0.0130157470703125, 0.0248565673828125, 0.0266876220703125, 0.0011310577392578125, -0.0226287841796875, 0.0209503173828125, -0.0188140869140625, 0.0284576416015625, -0.030029296875, 0.00394439697265625, -0.008575439453125, -0.03839111328125, 0.008209228515625, -0.003452301025390625, -0.0140228271484375, -0.00528717041015625, 0.050201416015625, 0.01788330078125, 0.0123443603515625, -0.0166168212890625, 0.00836181640625, 0.00960540771484375, 0.0009455680847167969, 0.0093841552734375, 0.00377655029296875, -0.031005859375, 0.00691986083984375, -0.0265655517578125, -0.0030269622802734375, 0.0025196075439453125, -0.0313720703125, 0.018707275390625, -0.038360595703125, 0.006374359130859375, -0.026824951171875, -0.0149688720703125, 0.0179901123046875, -0.0217742919921875, -0.00963592529296875, -0.011322021484375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "\"\"\"\n# Introduction\n\nMemory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. In these systems, a *textual memory bank* refers to a structured collection of text-based experiences, solutions, or feedback that the agent accumulates and references to inform future decisions. A *self-improving loop* is the iterative process where the agent uses its memory to refine its strategies and performance over successive tasks. However, the reliability aspects of these methods have been critically overlooked.\n\n# Methodology\n\nIn this work, we conduct a comprehensive re-evaluation of two representative memory-based methods. We broaden the scope of evaluation along two axes: (1) including multiple independent runs to quantify performance variance, and (2) randomly shuffling the order of tasks to investigate the effect of task sequencing. For each experimental condition, we measure agent performance on complex, multi-step tasks in simulated environments, recording both average outcomes and variability across runs.\n\nTo further probe the causes of observed fragility, we manually examine the agents' memory contents and introduce interventions aimed at reducing underspecification. Specifically, we augment the memory construction process with *detailed rubrics*\u2014explicit criteria for task success\u2014and *environment feedback*\u2014additional signals from the environment indicating partial progress or errors. These enhancements are implemented by providing the agent with structured checklists and real-time feedback messages during task execution.\n\n# Results\n\nOur experiments yield two key observations that expose the fragility of current methods. First, agent evaluation is inherently noisy in complex environments and on multi-step tasks; introducing a self-improving loop can further amplify this noise, as errors or suboptimal strategies may be reinforced over time. Second, the agent's improvement is highly dependent on task order. Prior works often adopt default orderings that impose an implicit curriculum, which acts as a hidden prerequisite for success. When task order is randomized, performance frequently degrades, revealing a lack of robustness.\n\n# Discussion\n\nManual inspection of agent memory suggests that task and environment underspecification\u2014where instructions or feedback lack sufficient detail\u2014contributes to this fragility. To validate this hypothesis, we compare agent performance with and without the inclusion of detailed rubrics and environment feedback. Success is measured by the agent's ability to generalize improvements across varied task orders and by reductions in performance variance. While these interventions partially mitigate performance degradation, persistent issues remain: agents still struggle with ambiguous instructions, incomplete feedback, and unexpected environment dynamics. These *gaps* highlight the need for further research into more comprehensive specification and robust learning mechanisms.\n\n# Conclusion\n\nLooking ahead, our work advocates for more rigorous evaluation protocols for self-improving agents. Specifically, we recommend: (1) reporting results across multiple independent runs to capture variance, (2) evaluating agents under randomized and adversarial task orderings, and (3) explicitly documenting any curricula or ordering assumptions. Additionally, our findings on underspecification call for the development of systems and interfaces that enable effective human oversight. Such systems could include interactive dashboards for monitoring agent memory, tools for injecting clarifying feedback, and mechanisms for humans to audit or correct agent reasoning in real time. These features would help prevent agents from failing in unforeseeable ways and support safer deployment in real-world settings."}}